/* Topic 1 & 2 question bank */
const ref=r=>{QB[QB.length-1].ref=r};

/* ===================== TOPIC 1: Spacecraft Environment & Design ===================== */
// ---- Tutorial 1 originals (keys from the official answer sheet)
M(1,"tut","What is the Karman Line?",["The boundary between Earth's atmosphere and outer space","The equatorial plane of a geostationary orbit","The orbit altitude of a polar satellite","The distance from Earth to the Moon"],0,"<p>The Kármán line is the conventional boundary between airspace and outer space: the theoretical limit of aeronautical (winged) flight. The FAI sets it at about <b>100 km (62.5 mi)</b>.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q1");
M(1,"tut","Which organization defines the Karman Line?",["NASA","FAI (Federation Aeronautique Internationale)","NACA (National Advisory Committee on Aeronautics)","U.S. Military"],1,"<p>The <b>FAI</b> uses the Kármán line (~100 km). NACA, NASA and the U.S. Military use a different boundary: <b>50 miles (80 km)</b>.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q2");
M(1,"tut","What is the primary factor that distinguishes an equatorial orbit?",["High altitude","Low inclination","Long orbital period","Fast satellite speed"],1,"<p>An equatorial orbit lies in the equatorial plane, so its inclination is <b>≈ 0°</b>. Altitude, period and speed don't define it: an equatorial orbit can be at any altitude.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q3");
M(1,"tut","What type of orbit is ideal for global observations and has a short revisit time?",["Equatorial Orbit","Polar Orbit","Inclined Orbit","Circular Orbit"],1,"<p>A polar orbit (i ≈ 90°) passes over both poles while Earth rotates beneath it, so over time it sees the <b>whole surface</b> and revisits regions frequently.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q4");
M(1,"tut","Which Kepler's law describes the shape of an orbit as an ellipse?",["Kepler's First Law (Law of Ellipses)","Kepler's Second Law (Law of Areas)","Kepler's Third Law (Harmonic Law)","Kepler's Law of Gravitation"],0,"<p>First law: orbits are ellipses with the central body at <b>one focus</b>. Polar form: <code>r = a(1−e²)/(1+e·cosθ)</code>. (There is no \"Kepler's Law of Gravitation\" – gravitation is Newton's.)</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q5");
M(1,"tut","What is the primary difference between a circular orbit and an elliptical orbit?",["Altitude variation","Eccentricity","Orbital period","Velocity variation"],1,"<p><b>Eccentricity</b> is the defining parameter: e = 0 is a circle, 0 &lt; e &lt; 1 is an ellipse. Altitude and velocity variation are <i>consequences</i> of eccentricity, not the defining difference.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q6");
M(1,"tut","Which type of orbit is characterized by remaining stationary relative to a fixed point on the Earth's surface?",["Low Earth Orbit (LEO)","Medium Earth Orbit (MEO)","Geostationary Orbit (GEO)","Polar Orbit"],2,"<p>GEO: period equals Earth's rotation (~24 h), inclination 0°, circular, at 35,786 km – so the satellite hovers over one longitude on the equator.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q7");
M(1,"tut","What is the approximate altitude of a geostationary orbit?",["160 kilometers","2,000 kilometers","35,786 kilometers","80 kilometers"],2,"<p><b>35,786 km (22,236 miles)</b> above the equator. The distractors are real course numbers too: 160 km = lowest practical orbit / LEO lower bound, 2,000 km = LEO/MEO boundary, 80 km = the 50-mile NASA/US military space boundary.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q8");
M(1,"tut","Which orbit type is known for its low levels of radiation exposure due to proximity to Earth's atmosphere?",["Geostationary Orbit (GEO)","Low Earth Orbit (LEO)","Medium Earth Orbit (MEO)","Polar Orbit"],1,"<p>LEO sits below the Van Allen belts and is shielded by Earth's magnetic field (the course words it as \"proximity to Earth's protective atmosphere\").</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q9");
M(1,"tut","What is the inclination angle of a polar orbit?",["0°","45°","90°","180°"],2,"<p>Polar ≈ <b>90°</b> (plane perpendicular to the equator). 0° = equatorial prograde; 180° = equatorial retrograde.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q10");
M(1,"tut","Which law describes the rate at which the radius vector of an orbiting object sweeps out equal areas in equal times?",["Kepler's First Law (Law of Ellipses)","Kepler's Second Law (Law of Areas)","Kepler's Third Law (Harmonic Law)","Kepler's Law of Gravitation"],1,"<p>Second law: <code>dA/dt = h/2</code> (constant), where h is specific angular momentum. Consequence: fastest at periapsis, slowest at apoapsis.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q11");
M(1,"tut","What does the semi-major axis of an elliptical orbit represent?",["The degree of deviation from a perfect circle","The average distance from the center of the ellipse to the periapsis","The orbital period of the object","The altitude of the orbit"],1,"<p>Course key: the semi-major axis <i>a</i> is the distance from the <b>centre of the ellipse</b> to the periapsis (or apoapsis). Equivalently, a = (r<sub>p</sub> + r<sub>a</sub>)/2 – the average of closest and farthest distances from the focus. \"Deviation from a circle\" is eccentricity.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q12");
M(1,"tut","In which type of orbit does a satellite appear to be fixed at a specific point in the sky?",["Equatorial Orbit","Polar Orbit","Geostationary Orbit","Inclined Orbit"],2,"<p>Geostationary. Note the trap: \"equatorial\" alone is not enough – an equatorial orbit at 500 km is not fixed in the sky. It must be equatorial <i>and</i> at the 24-hour (35,786 km) altitude.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q13");
M(1,"tut","What is the primary advantage of Low Earth Orbit (LEO) for satellite constellations?",["Longer orbital periods","Higher radiation exposure","Shorter latency","Larger coverage area"],2,"<p>Being close to Earth means short signal paths → <b>low latency</b>. LEO has <i>shorter</i> periods, <i>lower</i> radiation, and a <i>smaller</i> footprint per satellite (that's why constellations need many satellites).</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q14");
M(1,"tut","Which organization placed human-occupied space stations like the International Space Station (ISS) in Low Earth Orbit (LEO)?",["NASA","ESA (European Space Agency)","Roscosmos (Russian Space Agency)","SpaceX"],0,"<p>Answer-sheet key: <b>NASA</b>. In reality the ISS is a partnership of NASA, Roscosmos, JAXA, ESA and CSA (Topic 2) – NASA leads it.</p>");ref("Tut 1 · Q15");

// ---- Tutorial 1 reworded / inverted / swapped
M(1,"var","The FAI places the Kármán line at approximately which altitude?",["50 km","80 km","100 km","160 km"],2,"<p>About <b>100 km (62.5 miles)</b>. The FAI's underlying range is 53–62.5 miles depending on factors; 100 km is the practical round figure.</p>");
M(1,"var","NACA, NASA and the U.S. Military consider anyone flying above which altitude to be an astronaut?",["45 km","50 miles (80 km)","62.5 miles (100 km)","100 miles (160 km)"],1,"<p><b>50 miles ≈ 80 km.</b> Derived from X-plane testing (X-15), where control had to switch from aerodynamic surfaces to reaction control around that height.</p>");
M(1,"var","The 50-mile (80 km) space boundary was derived from testing of which vehicle?",["Space Shuttle","X-15 experimental aircraft","Saturn V","SpaceShipTwo"],1,"<p>X-plane testing, especially the <b>X-15</b>, which had to transition its control systems from aeronautics to astronautics around 50 miles.</p>");
M(1,"var","The Kármán line is named after…",["Theodore von Kármán, aeronautical engineer","Johannes Kepler","Edwin Hubble","Robert Goddard"],0,"<p>Named after aeronautical engineer <b>Theodore von Kármán</b>.</p>");
M(1,"var","According to the notes, the FAI Kármán line ranges between ___ depending on various factors.",["45–50 miles","50–53 miles","53–62.5 miles","62.5–100 miles"],2,"<p><b>53–62.5 miles</b>; the FAI fixes it at ~100 km (62.5 mi) for practical reasons.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which orbit is chosen when the mission needs the SAME patch of Earth in view continuously?",["Polar LEO","Geostationary","Inclined MEO","Highly elliptical"],1,"<p>GEO keeps a fixed vantage point (continuous coverage). Polar LEO gives global coverage but passes over quickly.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which orbit is chosen when the mission needs to see EVERY part of the globe over time?",["Geostationary","Equatorial LEO","Polar","GEO constellation"],2,"<p>Polar: Earth rotates beneath the orbit plane so the ground track eventually covers all latitudes and longitudes. GEO is limited near the poles.</p>");
M(1,"var","An orbit with an inclination of 0° is called…",["Polar","Equatorial","Sun-synchronous","Retrograde"],1,"<p>i ≈ 0° → orbit lies in Earth's equatorial plane.</p>");
M(1,"var","An orbit with an inclination near 90° is called…",["Equatorial","Geostationary","Polar","Molniya"],2,"<p>i ≈ 90° → plane perpendicular to the equator, passes over the poles.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which of Kepler's laws relates orbital period to orbit size?",["First","Second","Third","None – that is Newton's"],2,"<p>Third (Harmonic) law: <code>T² = 4π²a³/(GM)</code>, i.e. T² ∝ a³.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which of these is NOT one of Kepler's laws?",["Law of Ellipses","Law of Areas","Harmonic Law","Law of Universal Gravitation"],3,"<p>Universal gravitation is <b>Newton's</b> law. Kepler's three: Ellipses, Areas (equal areas in equal times), Harmonic (T² ∝ a³).</p>");
M(1,"var","Which parameter tells you how much an orbit deviates from a perfect circle?",["Semi-major axis","Eccentricity","Inclination","True anomaly"],1,"<p>Eccentricity e: 0 = circle, 0&lt;e&lt;1 = ellipse; higher e = more elongated.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which orbit band experiences the LOWEST radiation, according to the course?",["LEO","MEO","GEO","They are equal"],0,"<p>Course ranking: LEO lowest, MEO higher, GEO higher still (\"MEO… generally lower than that in GEO\"). MEO actually sits inside the radiation belts.</p>");
M(1,"var","GEO satellites suffer from which communication disadvantage compared to LEO?",["Small footprint","High latency","High atmospheric drag","Short lifetime"],1,"<p>~35,786 km each way → ~0.24 s round trip ground–satellite–ground. GEO has a <i>large</i> footprint, <i>low</i> drag and <i>long</i> lifetimes.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which orbit offers the LARGEST coverage footprint per satellite?",["LEO","MEO","GEO","Polar LEO"],2,"<p>Higher altitude = larger footprint. A GEO satellite can cover a continent or even a hemisphere.</p>");
M(1,"var","Which human-occupied facility is the course's example of a LEO resident?",["Hubble","International Space Station","GPS","Galileo"],1,"<p>The ISS (≈ 400 km) – a laboratory for research and long-duration spaceflight effects.</p>");
M(1,"var","The time for a LEO satellite to complete one orbit is typically…",["About 24 hours","A few to several hours","About 90–120 minutes","About 12 hours"],2,"<p>LEO and polar orbits: <b>90–120 min</b>. MEO: few–several hours (GPS ≈ 12 h). GEO: ~24 h.</p>");

// ---- Concepts: space environment
M(1,"con","The course defines space as…",["Everything above 160 km","All the reaches of the universe beyond Earth's atmosphere","The region beyond the Moon","The region above the Van Allen belts"],1,"<p>\"Space is defined as all the reaches of the universe beyond Earth's atmosphere.\" There is no definitive boundary; for atmospheric flight vehicles the limit may be as low as <b>45 km</b>.</p>");
M(1,"con","In terms of the limiting altitude for vehicles designed for atmospheric flight, the slides say space may be considered to begin as low as…",["45 km","80 km","100 km","160 km"],0,"<p>45 km (slide 2). Different definitions: 45 km (atmospheric-flight limit), 80 km (US), 100 km (FAI), 160 km (lowest practical orbit).</p>");
M(1,"con","What is the lowest practical orbit for an artificial satellite around Earth?",["About 80 km (50 mi)","About 100 km (62.5 mi)","About 160 km (100 mi)","About 400 km (250 mi)"],2,"<p>About <b>160 km (100 miles)</b> – below that, drag decays the orbit almost immediately. This is also the LEO lower bound.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which phrase from the notes describes space's particle density?",["A dense plasma","A near-perfect vacuum with very few particles","An atmosphere thinner than Mars","A uniform gas of hydrogen"],1,"<p>Near-perfect vacuum → extreme cold, near-zero pressure, no air, objects move without significant air resistance.</p>");
M(1,"con","Who first theorized the expansion of the universe, per the notes?",["Albert Einstein","Edwin Hubble","Johannes Kepler","Theodore von Kármán"],1,"<p>Cosmic Expansion – galaxies moving away from each other – \"first theorized by Edwin Hubble\".</p>");
M(1,"con","Space and time interconnected in a four-dimensional concept that mass can bend is from…",["Newton's law of gravitation","Einstein's general relativity","Kepler's harmonic law","Hubble's law"],1,"<p>Space-time, from Einstein's general theory of relativity; massive objects warp it.</p>");
M(1,"con","The interstellar medium is primarily made of…",["Dark matter","Gas and dust","Comets","Radiation only"],1,"<p>Matter (mainly gas and dust) between stars, from which new stars and planetary systems form.</p>");
M(1,"con","Select ALL of the cosmic phenomena listed in the notes.",["Supernovae","Black holes","Pulsars","Gamma-ray bursts","Cosmic background radiation","Aurora borealis"],[0,1,2,3,4],"<p>The notes list supernovae, black holes, pulsars, gamma-ray bursts and cosmic background radiation. Aurorae are an atmospheric effect, not in the list.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which Star Trek link to real spaceflight is mentioned in the notes?",["Bezos took a cameo in Star Trek Beyond (2016) and Alexa drew on the Enterprise computer","Branson named Blue Origin after Star Trek","NASA named the ISS 'Enterprise'","Bezos named his 2014 rocket Enterprise"],0,"<p>Bezos: cameo in <i>Star Trek Beyond</i> (2016), Alexa inspired by the Enterprise's computer. Branson named his 2014 rocket (spaceship) after the <i>Enterprise</i>. The opening quote is Captain Kirk's \"To boldly go where no man has gone before!\"</p>");
M(1,"fig","Use the chart. Which flight crossed the FAI Kármán line?",["Jeff Bezos's Blue Origin flight (107 km)","Richard Branson's Virgin Galactic flight (86 km)","Both","Neither"],0,"<p>Bezos 107 km &gt; 100 km. Branson 86 km &lt; 100 km, but &gt; 80 km so it counts under the NACA/NASA/US-military definition. Both counted as spaceflight under the 50-mile rule; only Bezos under the FAI rule.</p>",["S1_02.jpg","What qualifies as a space flight"]);
M(1,"fig","From the chart: Branson's flight reached 86 km. Under which definition(s) was it a spaceflight?",["FAI only","NACA/NASA/US Military 50-mile line only","Both definitions","Neither definition"],1,"<p>86 km is above 80 km (50 mi) but below 100 km (62.5 mi). So it qualifies under the US definition only.</p>",["S1_02.jpg","Altitudes: 107, 100, 86, 80 km"]);

// ---- Concepts: orbits by inclination
M(1,"con","The notes call the equatorial orbit also a…",["Sun-synchronous orbit","Geostationary or geosynchronous orbit","Molniya orbit","Transfer orbit"],1,"<p>The notes treat \"equatorial orbit\" as geostationary/geosynchronous: above the equator, matching Earth's rotation (~24 h, 35,786 km).</p>");
M(1,"con","The equatorial (geostationary) orbit altitude in miles is about…",["22,236 miles","1,242 miles","62.5 miles","239,000 miles"],0,"<p>35,786 km = <b>22,236 miles</b>. (1,242 mi = 2,000 km; 62.5 mi = Kármán line; 239,000 mi = Moon distance.)</p>");
M(1,"con","The notes give the band 'About 2,000 km – 35,786 km' at the start of the orbit types section. That band corresponds to…",["LEO","MEO","GEO","HEO"],1,"<p>2,000 – 35,786 km is MEO. Below 2,000 km is LEO; at 35,786 km is GEO.</p>");
M(1,"con","A polar orbit's plane is oriented ___ to Earth's equatorial plane.",["parallel","perpendicular","at 45°","at 23.5°"],1,"<p>Perpendicular (i ≈ 90°), so Earth rotates beneath it and the satellite eventually sees the whole surface.</p>");
M(1,"con","Why is a polar satellite's speed higher than a satellite in a higher orbit?",["Because i = 90° adds speed","Because it is at a lower altitude (smaller R in v = √(GM/R))","Because it crosses the poles where gravity is stronger","Because it is retrograde"],1,"<p>Speed comes from <b>altitude</b>, not inclination. Polar orbits are typically low, so R is small and v = √(GM/R) is larger.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which applications does the course list for polar orbits? (select all)",["Remote sensing","Weather forecasting","Environmental monitoring","Disaster management","Scientific research","Fixed-dish TV broadcasting"],[0,1,2,3,4],"<p>Polar: remote sensing, weather forecasting, environmental monitoring, disaster management, scientific research, climate studies. Fixed-dish TV needs GEO.</p>");
M(1,"con","The inclination angle (i) is defined as the angle between…",["The orbit plane and the equatorial plane of the central body","The satellite velocity and the radius vector","The perigee and the ascending node","The orbit plane and the ecliptic"],0,"<p>Inclination = angle between the orbital plane and the reference (equatorial) plane. (Perigee–node angle is the argument of perigee, Topic 5.)</p>");
M(1,"con","According to the notes, an inclined orbit gives the most varied coverage of different parts of Earth when its inclination is…",["Close to 0°","Close to 90°","Neither close to 0° nor 90°","Exactly 180°"],2,"<p>\"Especially if the inclination is neither close to 0° nor 90°.\" Close to 90° → polar; close to 0° → equatorial.</p>");
M(1,"con","What does inclination mainly affect, according to the notes?",["Orbital speed of a circular orbit","The geometry and ground track (which regions are passed over)","The satellite's mass","The gravitational constant"],1,"<p>Inclination determines the tilt of the orbital plane → ground track and coverage. It doesn't directly enter v = √(GM/R).</p>");
TF(1,"trap","For a circular orbit, changing the inclination from 0° to 90° (same altitude) changes the orbital speed.",false,"<p><b>False.</b> The notes say inclination \"doesn't have a direct impact on the velocity in a circular orbit\". v depends only on G, M and R.</p><p>Trap: the <i>inclined-orbit</i> slide also claims \"orbital period and velocity are influenced by the inclination\". Physically, for a given radius, they aren't. (What inclination <i>does</i> change is launch cost: Earth's rotation helps a low-inclination launch from the equator most.)</p>");

// ---- Velocity formula & variables
M(1,"con","In v = √(GM/R), what is R?",["Earth's radius only","Altitude above the surface only","Distance from Earth's centre: R = R_Earth + h","Semi-major axis of the transfer orbit"],2,"<p>R is measured from the <b>centre</b> of the Earth: R = R<sub>Earth</sub> + h, with R<sub>Earth</sub> ≈ 6,371 km. Forgetting to add Earth's radius is the classic mistake.</p>",["S1_14.jpg","R = R_Earth + h"]);
M(1,"con","Which value does the course use for the gravitational constant G?",["9.81 m/s²","6.67430 × 10⁻¹¹ m³/(kg·s²)","3.986 × 10¹⁴ m³/s²","5.972 × 10²⁴ kg"],1,"<p>G = 6.67430×10⁻¹¹ m³ kg⁻¹ s⁻². 9.81 is surface g; 3.986×10¹⁴ is GM for Earth (μ); 5.972×10²⁴ kg is Earth's mass.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which value is Earth's mass M in the course formulas?",["6.371 × 10⁶ kg","5.972 × 10²⁴ kg","3.986 × 10¹⁴ kg","6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ kg"],1,"<p>M<sub>Earth</sub> = 5.972×10²⁴ kg. (6.371×10⁶ <i>m</i> is Earth's radius.)</p>");
M(1,"fig","This slide shows the velocity formula for an equatorial orbit. Which statement about it is correct?",["It only applies to equatorial orbits","The same formula applies to equatorial, polar, inclined and circular orbits","It applies only above 35,786 km","It includes the satellite's mass"],1,"<p>The notes repeat: \"the formula … is the same as for equatorial, polar and inclined orbits\" – any circular orbit. The satellite's own mass cancels out.</p>",["S1_09.jpg","v = √(GM/R)"]);
M(1,"con","If Earth's mass were 4× larger (same orbit radius), circular orbital speed would…",["Double","Quadruple","Halve","Stay the same"],0,"<p>v ∝ √M → √4 = 2×.</p>");
M(1,"con","If an orbit radius R (from Earth's centre) is made 4× larger, circular orbital speed…",["Doubles","Halves","Quarters","Stays the same"],1,"<p>v ∝ 1/√R → 1/√4 = ½. Higher orbits are slower.</p>");
M(1,"con","Doubling the SATELLITE's mass changes its circular orbital speed by…",["×2","×√2","×½","No change"],3,"<p>The satellite's mass cancels (GMm/R² = mv²/R). Only the central mass M appears.</p>");

// ---- Kepler formulas & figures
M(1,"fig","In Kepler's first-law equation r = a(1−e²)/(1+e·cosθ), what is θ?",["Inclination","True anomaly – angle measured from periapsis to the object","Argument of periapsis","Mean anomaly"],1,"<p>θ is the <b>true anomaly</b>, measured from periapsis (closest point) to the object's position. At θ = 0, r = a(1−e) (periapsis); at θ = 180°, r = a(1+e) (apoapsis).</p>",["S1_19.jpg","Kepler's First Law in polar form"]);
M(1,"fig","In Kepler's second law dA/dt = h/2, what is h?",["Altitude","Specific angular momentum","Planck's constant","Height of the ellipse"],1,"<p>h = specific angular momentum (angular momentum per unit mass, r × v). Because h is constant, the area rate is constant.</p>",["S1_20.jpg","Law of Areas"]);
M(1,"fig","In T² = 4π²a³/(GM), which variable is NOT in the formula?",["Semi-major axis","Gravitational constant","Mass of the central body","Mass of the orbiting satellite"],3,"<p>T depends only on a and GM of the central body. The satellite mass is negligible and doesn't appear.</p>",["S1_21.jpg","Kepler's Third Law"]);
M(1,"fig","Panel (2) in the figure: a planet takes 6 months to cover each shaded area. What does this demonstrate?",["Orbits are ellipses","Equal areas in equal times – faster near the Sun","T² ∝ a³","Orbits are circles"],1,"<p>Kepler's 2nd law: equal areas in equal times, so the planet moves faster near perihelion and slower near aphelion.</p>",["S1_18.jpg","Kepler's 3 Laws of Planetary Motion"]);
M(1,"fig","In the Kepler diagram, the Sun is placed at…",["The centre of the ellipse","One focus of the ellipse","Both foci","The aphelion"],1,"<p>One focus. The other focus is empty.</p>",["S1_22.jpg","Kepler's First/Second/Third law diagrams"]);

// ---- Orbit shape
M(1,"con","What provides the centripetal acceleration that keeps a satellite in a circular orbit?",["Thrusters firing continuously","Gravitational force between satellite and central body","Atmospheric lift","Solar radiation pressure"],1,"<p>Gravity supplies the centripetal force: GMm/R² = mv²/R → v = √(GM/R).</p>");
M(1,"con","For an elliptical orbit, 0 &lt; e &lt; 1. What is e for a circle?",["0","0.5","1","Greater than 1"],0,"<p>e = 0 circle; 0&lt;e&lt;1 ellipse; e = 1 parabola (escape); e &gt; 1 hyperbola.</p>");
M(1,"con","For an Earth orbit, the farthest point is called the…",["Perigee","Apogee","Periapsis","Node"],1,"<p>General terms: periapsis (closest) / apoapsis (farthest). Earth-specific: perigee / apogee.</p>");
M(1,"con","Where is an object in an elliptical orbit fastest?",["At apoapsis","At periapsis","At the ascending node","Speed is constant"],1,"<p>Fastest at periapsis, slowest at apoapsis – Kepler's 2nd law / conservation of angular momentum.</p>");
TF(1,"trap","An elliptical orbit has a longer period than a circular orbit with the SAME semi-major axis.",false,"<p><b>False in real physics</b>, though the notes state it. Kepler's 3rd law, T = 2π√(a³/GM), depends only on a – an ellipse and a circle with the same semi-major axis have the <b>same</b> period. Know what the notes say, but the physics answer is \"same period\".</p>");
TF(1,"trap","The notes give v = √(GM/r) for the speed at any point of an elliptical orbit. This is exact for ellipses.",false,"<p><b>False.</b> √(GM/r) is the circular speed at radius r. The exact speed on an ellipse is the vis-viva equation <code>v = √(GM(2/r − 1/a))</code>. They agree only when r = a (circle).</p>");
M(1,"fig","The figure shows Chandrayaan-3's path. Why is it the course's example of elliptical orbits?",["It flew a single circular orbit to the Moon","It raised a series of Earth-bound elliptical orbits, then a lunar transfer trajectory, then lunar orbits down to 100 × 100 km","It used a polar GEO","It used a solar sail"],1,"<p>Chandrayaan-3 (follow-on to Chandrayaan-2, demonstrating safe landing and roving): injection orbit → successive elliptical Earth-bound burns → lunar transfer trajectory → lunar orbit insertion → circularised 100 × 100 km lunar orbit.</p>",["S1_28.jpg","Chandrayaan-3 mission profile"]);
M(1,"con","According to the notes, a circular orbit's altitude above the surface 'may vary based on…'",["The inclination","The size of the central body","The eccentricity","Solar radiation"],1,"<p>Radius is constant, but the altitude for a given radius depends on the size (radius) of the central body.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which force does the course say balances gravity in a circular orbit (rotating-frame description)?",["Lift","Centrifugal force","Thrust","Drag"],1,"<p>\"The gravitational force … is balanced by the centrifugal force.\" (In an inertial frame we'd instead say gravity <i>provides</i> the centripetal force.)</p>");

// ---- Altitude classes
M(1,"con","LEO altitude range per the course:",["80–160 km","160–2,000 km","2,000–35,786 km","Exactly 35,786 km"],1,"<p>~160 km (100 mi) to 2,000 km (1,242 mi).</p>");
M(1,"con","Which constellations does the course name as the most prominent in MEO?",["Starlink and OneWeb","GPS and Galileo","Intelsat and Inmarsat","Landsat and Sentinel"],1,"<p>GPS & Galileo navigation constellations in MEO.</p>");
M(1,"con","Why are there no habitable space stations in MEO?",["MEO is too close to the atmosphere","MEO lies in the radiation belts – dangerous radiation for astronauts","MEO is too expensive to communicate with","Station-keeping is impossible in MEO"],1,"<p>The Van Allen radiation belts – charged particles from the solar wind trapped by Earth's magnetic field – make MEO dangerous for humans.</p>");
TF(1,"trap","The notes call the MEO radiation zone the 'Van Halen Radiation belt'. This is the correct scientific name.",false,"<p>It's the <b>Van Allen</b> radiation belts (after James Van Allen). Van Halen is a rock band – a typo in the notes. Expect the correct name in an exam.</p>");
M(1,"con","Which MEO feature contributes to longer satellite lifetimes than LEO?",["Higher radiation","More resilient to space debris and atmospheric drag","Shorter period","Lower latency"],1,"<p>Less drag and less debris than LEO → longer lifetimes.</p>");
M(1,"con","MEO is described as a balance between…",["LEO's low latency and GEO's large coverage","GEO's low latency and LEO's large coverage","Polar coverage and equatorial coverage","Low cost and high radiation"],0,"<p>MEO balances LEO's advantage (shorter latency) and GEO's (larger coverage area).</p>");
M(1,"con","GEO is also called…",["Geosynchronous Equatorial Orbit","Global Earth Orbit","Graveyard Earth Orbit","Geocentric Elliptic Orbit"],0,"<p>Geostationary Orbit = Geosynchronous Equatorial Orbit (both 'GEO').</p>");
M(1,"con","Select ALL GEO key features listed in the notes.",["Altitude ≈ 35,786 km","Period ≈ 24 h","Fixed position over a longitude","Less susceptible to drag → longer lifetime","Large footprint","High latency","Global polar coverage"],[0,1,2,3,4,5],"<p>The 7 listed features are altitude, period, fixed position, applications, satellite resilience, larger footprint and high latency. GEO is <i>limited</i> near the poles.</p>");
TF(1,"trap","Every geosynchronous orbit is geostationary.",false,"<p><b>False.</b> Geosynchronous = 24 h (sidereal) period. Geostationary additionally needs <b>i = 0°</b> and <b>e = 0</b>. An inclined geosynchronous satellite traces a figure-8 in the sky. The notes use the terms loosely.</p>");
TF(1,"trap","Astronauts float on the ISS because gravity there is almost zero.",false,"<p><b>False</b>, though the notes say gravity is \"much weaker in space\". At ISS altitude (~400 km) gravity is still ~90 % of surface gravity. Astronauts float because they are in continuous <b>free fall</b> around Earth – that is microgravity.</p>");

// ---- IMU
M(1,"con","An IMU measures…",["Position via GPS","Angular velocity and linear acceleration","Temperature and pressure","Solar radiation"],1,"<p>IMUs measure angular velocity (gyroscopes) and linear acceleration (accelerometers). Data drive thrusters/reaction wheels for attitude control.</p>");
M(1,"con","Why are IMUs especially useful in deep space?",["They need GPS","They track movement without external references, where GPS is unavailable","They generate power","They shield radiation"],1,"<p>They work without external references, so they function where GPS signals are unavailable.</p>");
M(1,"con","IMU data are used by onboard control systems to adjust…",["Solar panel coatings","Thrusters or reaction wheels","Battery chemistry","Antenna frequency"],1,"<p>To keep the spacecraft stable against gravitational pulls or atmospheric drag.</p>");

// ---- Calculations (fresh numbers each run)
G(1,"calc",()=>{const h=pick([ri(250,600),ri(600,1200),ri(1200,1990),400,20200,35786]);const v=Math.sqrt(MU/(RE+h*1e3));return{q:`Calculate the circular orbital velocity at an altitude of <b>${h.toLocaleString()} km</b>. Use R<sub>Earth</sub> = 6,371 km, M = 5.972×10²⁴ kg, G = 6.67430×10⁻¹¹ m³/kg/s².`,a:v,unit:"m/s",e:`<p>R = 6,371 + ${h.toLocaleString()} = ${(6371+h).toLocaleString()} km = ${sig((RE+h*1e3),5)} m</p><p>GM = 6.67430×10⁻¹¹ × 5.972×10²⁴ ≈ 3.986×10¹⁴ m³/s²</p><p>v = √(GM/R) = √(3.986×10¹⁴ / ${sig(RE+h*1e3,5)}) ≈ <b>${sig(v,4)} m/s</b></p><p>(Bonus Q21 in Tutorial 1 used h = 400 km → 7.66×10³ m/s.)</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const v=rnd(3100,7800);const R=MU/(v*v);const h=(R-RE)/1e3;return{q:`A satellite is in a circular Earth orbit moving at <b>${sig(v,4)} m/s</b>. What is its altitude above the surface? (R<sub>E</sub> = 6,371 km, GM = 3.986×10¹⁴ m³/s²)`,a:h,unit:"km",e:`<p>Rearrange v = √(GM/R) → R = GM/v² = 3.986×10¹⁴ / (${sig(v,4)})² = ${sig(R/1e3,5)} km</p><p>h = R − R<sub>E</sub> = ${sig(R/1e3,5)} − 6,371 = <b>${sig(h,4)} km</b></p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const h=pick([ri(200,1500),20200,35786,ri(2000,30000)]);const R=RE+h*1e3;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(R**3/MU)/60;return{q:`Using Kepler's third law T² = 4π²a³/(GM), find the period of a circular orbit at <b>${h.toLocaleString()} km</b> altitude.`,a:T,unit:"minutes",e:`<p>a = R<sub>E</sub> + h = ${sig(R/1e3,5)} km</p><p>T = 2π√(a³/GM) = 2π√((${sig(R,5)})³ / 3.986×10¹⁴) = ${sig(T*60,5)} s = <b>${sig(T,4)} min</b> (${sig(T/60,3)} h)</p><p>Check: LEO ≈ 90–120 min, GPS (20,200 km) ≈ 12 h, GEO ≈ 24 h.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const Th=pick([12,ri(2,20),23.934,6,8]);const T=Th*3600;const a=Math.cbrt(MU*T*T/(4*Math.PI**2));const h=(a-RE)/1e3;return{q:`A circular Earth orbit has period <b>${Th} hours</b>. Find its altitude (swap the unknown in Kepler's 3rd law).`,a:h,unit:"km",e:`<p>a = ∛(GM·T²/4π²) = ∛(3.986×10¹⁴ × ${sig(T,5)}² / 39.48) = ${sig(a/1e3,5)} km</p><p>h = a − 6,371 = <b>${sig(h,4)} km</b>${Th===23.934?" ← the geostationary altitude (one sidereal day).":""}</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const k=pick([2,3,4,1.5,0.5,9]);const f=Math.pow(k,1.5);return{k:"mcq",q:`Orbit B has a semi-major axis <b>${k}×</b> that of orbit A (same central body). How does B's period compare with A's?`,o:[`${sig(f,3)}× longer`,`${k}× longer`,`${sig(k*k,3)}× longer`,`${sig(Math.sqrt(k),3)}× longer`],a:0,e:`<p>T ∝ a<sup>3/2</sup> → T<sub>B</sub>/T<sub>A</sub> = ${k}<sup>1.5</sup> = <b>${sig(f,3)}</b>${k<1?" (i.e. shorter)":""}.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const a=ri(7000,30000),e=+rnd(0.05,0.7).toFixed(2),th=pick([0,30,60,90,120,180]);const r=a*(1-e*e)/(1+e*Math.cos(th*Math.PI/180));return{q:`An orbit has a = <b>${a.toLocaleString()} km</b> and e = <b>${e}</b>. Using r = a(1−e²)/(1+e·cosθ), find r at true anomaly θ = <b>${th}°</b>.`,a:r,unit:"km",e:`<p>r = ${a}(1 − ${e}²)/(1 + ${e}·cos${th}°) = ${sig(a*(1-e*e),5)}/${sig(1+e*Math.cos(th*Math.PI/180),4)} = <b>${sig(r,5)} km</b></p><p>Periapsis (θ=0): a(1−e) = ${sig(a*(1-e),5)} km · Apoapsis (θ=180°): a(1+e) = ${sig(a*(1+e),5)} km.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const hp=ri(200,1000),ha=pick([ri(2000,10000),35786,ri(20000,40000)]);const rp=6371+hp,ra=6371+ha;const e=(ra-rp)/(ra+rp);return{q:`A satellite has perigee altitude <b>${hp} km</b> and apogee altitude <b>${ha.toLocaleString()} km</b>. Find the eccentricity.`,a:e,unit:"",tol:0.02,e:`<p>Convert altitudes to radii: r<sub>p</sub> = ${rp} km, r<sub>a</sub> = ${ra} km.</p><p>a = (r<sub>p</sub>+r<sub>a</sub>)/2 = ${sig((rp+ra)/2,5)} km · e = (r<sub>a</sub>−r<sub>p</sub>)/(r<sub>a</sub>+r<sub>p</sub>) = <b>${sig(e,3)}</b></p><p>Mistake to avoid: using altitudes instead of radii.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const h=ri(200,1200);const R=RE+h*1e3;const hh=Math.sqrt(MU*R);const rate=hh/2;return{q:`For a circular orbit at ${h} km, the specific angular momentum is h = √(GM·R). Using Kepler's 2nd law dA/dt = h/2, what area does the radius vector sweep per second?`,a:rate,unit:"m²/s",e:`<p>R = ${sig(R,5)} m · h = √(3.986×10¹⁴ × R) = ${sig(hh,4)} m²/s</p><p>dA/dt = h/2 = <b>${sig(rate,4)} m²/s</b> – the same at every point of the orbit.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const h=pick([ri(81,99),ri(101,120),ri(60,79),100,80]);const fai=h>=100,us=h>=80;return{k:"mcq",q:`A suborbital flight reaches <b>${h} km</b>. Which definitions call it a spaceflight?`,o:["Both FAI (100 km) and NACA/NASA/US military (80 km)","Only NACA/NASA/US military (80 km)","Only FAI (100 km)","Neither"],a:fai&&us?0:us?1:fai?2:3,e:`<p>FAI Kármán line: 100 km (62.5 mi). US line: 80 km (50 mi). ${h} km is ${fai?"above":"below"} 100 and ${us?"above":"below"} 80.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const mi=pick([50,62.5,100,1242,22236,ri(100,5000)]);return{q:`Convert <b>${mi.toLocaleString()} miles</b> to kilometres (1 mile = 1.609 km).`,a:mi*1.609344,unit:"km",e:`<p>${mi} × 1.609 = <b>${sig(mi*1.609344,4)} km</b>. Course pairs: 50 mi = 80 km, 62.5 mi ≈ 100 km, 100 mi ≈ 160 km, 1,242 mi ≈ 2,000 km, 22,236 mi = 35,786 km.</p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const b=pick([["the Moon",7.342e22,1737.4],["Mars",6.417e23,3389.5]]);const h=ri(100,500);const v=Math.sqrt(Gc*b[1]/((b[2]+h)*1e3));return{q:`Swap the central body: find the circular orbital speed ${h} km above <b>${b[0]}</b> (M = ${sig(b[1],4)} kg, radius = ${b[2]} km).`,a:v,unit:"m/s",e:`<p>Same formula, different M and R: R = ${b[2]} + ${h} = ${sig(b[2]+h,5)} km</p><p>v = √(GM/R) = √(6.6743×10⁻¹¹ × ${sig(b[1],4)} / ${sig((b[2]+h)*1e3,5)}) = <b>${sig(v,4)} m/s</b></p>`}});
G(1,"calc",()=>{const h=pick([550,1200,20200,35786]);const t=2*h*1e3/cL*1000;return{q:`What is the minimum ground→satellite→ground signal delay (straight up and back down, speed of light 3×10⁸ m/s) for a satellite at <b>${h.toLocaleString()} km</b>?`,a:t,unit:"ms",e:`<p>Path = 2 × ${h.toLocaleString()} km = ${sig(2*h,5)} km. t = d/c = <b>${sig(t,3)} ms</b>. This is why GEO has high latency (~240 ms one hop) and LEO low.</p>`}});

// ---- Open-ended (Tutorial 1 16–20 + extra)
O(1,"open","(Tutorial 1 Q16) Explain the significance of the Kármán Line in defining the boundary between airspace and outer space. How do the FAI and NACA/NASA/US Military define this boundary, and what are the implications for spaceflights?","<ul><li>Kármán line (FAI): theoretical limit of air flight; jurisdictional boundary between airspace and space; range 53–62.5 mi, set at ~100 km (62.5 mi). Named after Theodore von Kármán.</li><li>NACA/NASA/US Military: astronauts are people who fly above 50 miles (80 km), from X-15 testing where control moved from aerodynamic surfaces to reaction control.</li><li>Implications: Bezos (107 km) qualifies under both; Branson (86 km) only under the 50-mile rule. The definition decides astronaut status, records and legal jurisdiction.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 1 · Q16");
O(1,"open","(Tutorial 1 Q17) Describe the key features of an equatorial orbit (orbit plane, altitude, applications). How does satellite speed relate to Earth's rotation?","<ul><li>Plane aligned with Earth's equator, i ≈ 0°.</li><li>Altitude ≈ 35,786 km (22,236 mi); period ≈ 24 h.</li><li>Appears fixed at one point in the sky.</li><li>Uses: communication, weather monitoring, Earth observation, broadcasting.</li><li>Speed matches Earth's rotation rate, so it stays above the same point. v = √(GM/R).</li></ul>");ref("Tut 1 · Q17");
O(1,"open","(Tutorial 1 Q18) Compare and contrast polar and inclined orbits, their applications, and how inclination influences coverage and ground track.","<ul><li>Polar: i ≈ 90°, plane perpendicular to equator, low altitude, 90–120 min period, whole-Earth coverage over time, short revisit time; remote sensing, weather, climate, disaster management.</li><li>Inclined: plane tilted by i to the equator; near 90° → polar-like, near 0° → equatorial-like; intermediate values cover different latitude bands; remote sensing, EO, comms, science.</li><li>Inclination sets the highest latitude reached (= i) and shapes the ground track; it doesn't change circular speed.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 1 · Q18");
O(1,"open","(Tutorial 1 Q19) Explain Kepler's three laws and their significance in celestial mechanics and orbital dynamics.","<ul><li>1st (Ellipses): orbit is an ellipse with the central body at one focus; r = a(1−e²)/(1+e cosθ).</li><li>2nd (Areas): radius vector sweeps equal areas in equal times; dA/dt = h/2; faster at periapsis.</li><li>3rd (Harmonic): T² ∝ a³; T² = 4π²a³/GM.</li><li>Significance: predict position and period, design trajectories, choose orbit altitude for a required period (e.g. GEO).</li></ul>");ref("Tut 1 · Q19");
O(1,"open","(Tutorial 1 Q20) Discuss GEO's key features, applications, advantages and disadvantages. How does altitude affect coverage area and latency?","<ul><li>35,786 km, 24 h period, equatorial, fixed over one longitude.</li><li>Uses: comms, TV broadcasting, weather, EO.</li><li>Advantages: constant coverage of one region, fixed ground antennas, large footprint (continent/hemisphere), little drag → long life.</li><li>Disadvantages: high latency (~0.24 s up-down), poor polar coverage, expensive to reach.</li><li>Higher altitude → bigger footprint but longer signal path.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 1 · Q20");
O(1,"open","Build a comparison of LEO, MEO and GEO covering: altitude, period, radiation, latency, footprint, drag/debris, and a typical user.","<ul><li>LEO: 160–2,000 km; 90–120 min; lowest radiation; lowest latency; small footprint; most drag & debris; ISS, EO, Starlink.</li><li>MEO: 2,000–35,786 km; hours (GPS ~12 h); higher radiation (Van Allen belts); medium; medium; resilient; GPS, Galileo.</li><li>GEO: 35,786 km; ~24 h; highest per course; ~240 ms hop; continent/hemisphere; little drag; TV, weather, comms.</li></ul>");
O(1,"open","Explain why an IMU is central to spacecraft stability, and what its limitations would be on a long mission.","<ul><li>Measures angular velocity and linear acceleration with gyros and accelerometers; feeds control algorithms that command thrusters/reaction wheels; keeps instruments and antennas pointed; works without external references (deep space).</li><li>Limitation (beyond the notes): integrated errors drift over time, so IMUs are combined with star trackers or sun sensors to reset attitude.</li></ul>");

/* ===================== TOPIC 2: Intro to Spacecraft Systems ===================== */
M(2,"tut","What is the primary function of Payload Systems in spacecraft?",["Maneuvering spacecraft","Generating solar power","Controlling and communicating spacecraft payloads","Conducting scientific experiments"],2,"<p>Payload Systems = radio technologies on the spacecraft <i>plus</i> ground equipment for controlling payloads and communicating results to mission control.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q1");
M(2,"tut","What role do microwave radio signals play in space communication?",["Navigation signals","Power generation","Remote sensing tools","Communication backbone"],3,"<p>\"Microwave radio signals serve as the backbone of communication between space systems and the ground.\" They <i>also</i> act as remote-sensing tools and navigation signals, but the backbone role is the primary one asked about.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q2");
M(2,"tut","Which type of satellite is used for weather monitoring and studying meteorological parameters?",["Communication satellite","Weather satellite","Navigation satellite","Scientific satellite"],1,"<p>Weather satellites: cloud cover, atmospheric conditions, precipitation. Example: NOAA satellites.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q3");
M(2,"tut","What is the purpose of CubeSats in space exploration?",["Deep space exploration","Large-scale communication","Cost-effective satellite design","Military surveillance"],2,"<p>CubeSats: standardized 10 cm cubes (1U) → compact, low cost, rapid development, constellations.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q4");
M(2,"tut","Which technology involves using a nuclear reactor to heat a propellant for propulsion?",["CubeSats","Solar sails","Nuclear Thermal Propulsion (NTP)","Ion Engines"],2,"<p>NTP heats a propellant such as hydrogen in a reactor and expels it → much higher specific impulse than chemical rockets.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q5");
M(2,"tut","What is the main advantage of Solar Sails for space missions?",["High thrust","Low cost","Rapid development","Long-duration propulsion"],3,"<p>Sails give continuous, low-thrust propulsion without propellant → long-duration and deep-space missions. Thrust is <i>low</i>, not high.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q6");
M(2,"tut","What is the primary source of power for many spacecraft?",["Nuclear energy","Solar radiation","Battery packs","Radioactive decay"],1,"<p>Solar panels (photovoltaic cells) convert sunlight into electricity. Batteries <i>store</i> energy for eclipses; RTGs are used far from the Sun.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q7");
M(2,"tut","Which feature distinguishes the International Space Station (ISS) from other satellites?",["Habitable environment","Geostationary orbit","Weather monitoring","Military applications"],0,"<p>The ISS is a <b>habitable</b> artificial satellite. It is in LEO (~400 km), not GEO.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q8");
M(2,"tut","What is the primary role of military satellites?",["Scientific research","Weather forecasting","Defense and national security","Communication services"],2,"<p>Defense and national security – surveillance, secure comms, navigation, early warning, etc.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q9");
M(2,"tut","What is the primary function of telemetry, tracking, and telecommand (TT&C) in Payload Systems?",["Power generation","Earth observation","Controlling and communicating spacecraft payloads","Weather monitoring"],2,"<p>TT&C is the ground-segment part: telemetry (data down), tracking (where it is), telecommand (commands up), including signal coding, modulation and RF equipment.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q10");
M(2,"tut","What is the main challenge associated with Nuclear Thermal Propulsion (NTP)?",["Low efficiency","Safety concerns","Limited thrust","High development cost"],1,"<p>Safety concerns, engineering complexity, handling nuclear materials. NTP has <i>high</i> efficiency and thrust.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q11");
M(2,"tut","Which radiation type is part of the ionizing radiation spectrum?",["Radio waves","Infrared rays","Ultraviolet rays","Microwaves"],2,"<p>Ionizing per course: UV, X-rays, gamma rays. Radio, microwave and IR are non-ionizing.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q12");
M(2,"tut","What does Solar Radiation Spectrum include that contributes to ionization?",["Gravitational waves","Cosmic rays","Neutrinos","Infrared radiation"],1,"<p>Answer key: cosmic rays. Strictly, cosmic rays are high-energy <i>particles from outside the solar system</i>, not part of the Sun's EM spectrum – but they are listed with solar wind as sources of ionizing radiation in space.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q13");
M(2,"tut","What is the primary effect of ionizing radiation on spacecraft electronics?",["Increased efficiency","Enhanced durability","Temporary or permanent malfunction","Improved performance"],2,"<p>Single Event Effects: one particle hitting a sensitive spot causes a temporary or permanent malfunction.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q14");
M(2,"tut","What is a key consideration in designing spacecraft for long-duration missions in regions with low sunlight?",["Increased solar panel efficiency","Implementation of ion thrusters","Energy storage solutions","Enhanced thermal radiators"],2,"<p>Rechargeable batteries / energy storage keep systems running through eclipse and darkness.</p>");ref("Tut 2 · Q15");

// reworded
M(2,"var","The word 'payload' originally comes from…",["Aviation","Seafaring – revenue-producing cargo","Railways","Military artillery"],1,"<p>From seafaring: the cargo that earned revenue. In space: the elements that produce mission data and relay it to Earth.</p>");
M(2,"var","In space terms, the payload is the part of the spacecraft dedicated to…",["Producing mission data and relaying it to Earth","Generating thrust","Holding the structure together","Thermal control"],0,"<p>\"Spacecraft elements dedicated to producing mission data and relaying it to Earth.\"</p>");
M(2,"var","Which tutorial technology had 'safety concerns' as its main challenge?",["CubeSats","Solar sails","Nuclear Thermal Propulsion","Ion engines"],2,"<p>NTP – nuclear materials. CubeSats: limited payload/power/comms. Sails: deployment & orientation. Ion: low thrust & power.</p>");
M(2,"var","Match: 'Low thrust limits initial acceleration; needs lots of power' is the challenge of…",["NTP","Ion engines","CubeSats","Solar sails"],1,"<p>Ion engines (electric propulsion): high efficiency, very low thrust, power-hungry.</p>");
M(2,"var","Match: 'Deployment and maintaining orientation to sunlight' is the challenge of…",["Solar sails","NTP","CubeSats","Ion engines"],0,"<p>Solar sails must unfurl huge thin sails and keep them angled to the Sun.</p>");
M(2,"var","Match: 'Limited payload capacity, power and communication' is the challenge of…",["Ion engines","CubeSats","NTP","Solar sails"],1,"<p>CubeSats – small size limits payload, power, comms.</p>");
M(2,"var","Which of these is NOT ionizing radiation per the course?",["Ultraviolet","X-rays","Gamma rays","Microwaves"],3,"<p>Microwaves are non-ionizing (and are the comms backbone).</p>");
M(2,"var","What is the main purpose of energy storage on a LEO spacecraft?",["Increase thrust","Keep systems powered during eclipse (shadow) phases","Shield radiation","Reduce mass"],1,"<p>LEO spacecraft pass in and out of Earth's shadow every orbit; batteries cover eclipse.</p>");
M(2,"var","Which component of Payload Systems belongs to the ground segment?",["Synthetic aperture radar","TT&C (telemetry, tracking & telecommand)","Solar arrays","Radiometer"],1,"<p>TT&C, signal coding/modulation and RF equipment form the Earth-based ground segment.</p>");

// payload systems detail
M(2,"con","Which navigation systems does the course list that Payload Systems must handle? (select all)",["GPS","GLONASS","EGNOS","Galileo","Starlink"],[0,1,2,3],"<p>GPS, GLONASS, Europe's land-based EGNOS overlay signal, and the Galileo system. Starlink is a broadband constellation.</p>");
M(2,"con","EGNOS is described as Europe's…",["Land-based overlay signal","Mars orbiter","Telecom satellite bus","Radar satellite"],0,"<p>EGNOS: land-based overlay that improves GPS accuracy over Europe.</p>");
M(2,"con","Which ESA-fostered class of telecom satellites has enhanced onboard power and data handling?",["Alphabus","Proba","Sentinel","EarthCARE"],0,"<p>Alphabus.</p>");
M(2,"con","Which two missions are cited as needing extremely accurate positioning control from onboard radio-navigation receivers?",["GOCE and LISA Pathfinder","Rosetta and Planck","SMOS and EarthCARE","Hubble and Landsat"],0,"<p>GOCE and LISA Pathfinder.</p>");
M(2,"con","Proba-3 is…",["A two-satellite technology demonstrator for precise formation flying","A comet lander","A soil-moisture radiometer","A GEO telecom satellite"],0,"<p>Two-satellite formation-flying demonstrator.</p>");
M(2,"con","CONSERT and MIRO were radio-based sensors aboard which mission?",["Planck","Rosetta","GOCE","Sentinel-1"],1,"<p>Rosetta – studying cometary surfaces and surrounding dust.</p>");
M(2,"con","Which mission used a radiometer to map the Cosmic Microwave Background more accurately than ever before?",["SMOS","Planck","EarthCARE","Proba-3"],1,"<p>Planck.</p>");
M(2,"con","SMOS measures…",["Atmospheric clouds","Soil moisture on land and ocean salinity","Comet dust","Gravity field"],1,"<p>SMOS radiometer: soil moisture + ocean salinity.</p>");
M(2,"con","EarthCARE carries…",["A SAR","An atmospheric radar and radiometer","An ion engine","A solar sail"],1,"<p>Atmospheric radar and radiometer.</p>");
M(2,"con","Which satellite is destined to carry the next-generation SAR instrument?",["Sentinel-1","Planck","SMOS","Alphabus"],0,"<p>Sentinel-1 radar satellite.</p>");
M(2,"con","Which three microwave instrument types are named for science and Earth observation?",["SAR, radar altimeters, radiometers","Telescopes, spectrometers, cameras","Gyros, star trackers, sun sensors","Ion, Hall, gridded thrusters"],0,"<p>Synthetic aperture radar (SAR), radar altimeters and radiometers.</p>");
M(2,"con","Payload instruments operate on the radio spectrum up to…",["Infrared","Microwave or millimetre-wave frequencies","X-ray","Visible light"],1,"<p>\"Operating on the radio spectrum up to microwave or millimeter-wave frequencies.\"</p>");
M(2,"con","How do new technologies respond to rising data rates, per the notes?",["Larger solar panels","Signal coding and modulation for efficient spectrum use, plus transponders and amplifiers","Ion engines","Lower orbits only"],1,"<p>Signal coding/modulation for spectrum efficiency, transponders and amplifiers for reliable links over thousands of km.</p>");

// satellite basics
M(2,"con","The Moon's mean distance from Earth is…",["35,786 km","384,400 km (239,000 mi)","40 trillion km","150 million km"],1,"<p>384,400 km. Nearest stars: more than 40 trillion km (25 trillion mi).</p>");
M(2,"con","How far are Earth's nearest neighbouring stars, per the notes?",["4 million km","More than 40 trillion km (25 trillion mi)","384,400 km","1 billion km"],1,"<p>&gt; 40 trillion km.</p>");
M(2,"con","Match the satellite type to the course example: Landsat",["Communication","Weather","Earth observation","Scientific"],2,"<p>Landsat – Earth observation (environmental monitoring). Intelsat → comms; NOAA → weather; GPS → navigation; Hubble → scientific.</p>");
M(2,"con","Match the satellite type to the course example: Intelsat",["Communication","Navigation","Earth observation","Scientific"],0,"<p>Intelsat – global communications.</p>");
M(2,"con","Match the satellite type to the course example: Hubble Space Telescope",["Weather","Scientific","Military","Navigation"],1,"<p>Scientific – astronomy observations.</p>");
