// calculations
G(2,"calc",()=>{const v=pick([28000,27600,ri(26000,28500)]),T=pick([90,92,ri(88,96)]);const d=v*T/60;return{q:`The ISS moves at about <b>${v.toLocaleString()} km/h</b> and completes an orbit in <b>${T} min</b>. How far does it travel per orbit?`,a:d,unit:"km",e:`<p>d = v·t = ${v.toLocaleString()} × ${T}/60 = <b>${sig(d,4)} km</b>. Implied radius = d/2π ≈ ${sig(d/2/Math.PI,4)} km → altitude ≈ ${sig(d/2/Math.PI-6371,3)} km.</p>`}});
G(2,"calc",()=>{const U=pick([1,2,3,6,12]);return{q:`A <b>${U}U</b> CubeSat is made of 10 cm cubes. What is its volume?`,a:U,unit:"litres",e:`<p>1U = 10×10×10 cm = 1,000 cm³ = 1 L. ${U}U ≈ <b>${U} L</b>.</p>`}});
G(2,"calc",()=>{const mph=pick([17500,ri(15000,18000)]);return{q:`Convert <b>${mph.toLocaleString()} mph</b> to km/h.`,a:mph*1.609344,unit:"km/h",e:`<p>${mph} × 1.609 = <b>${sig(mph*1.609344,4)} km/h</b> (17,500 mph ≈ 28,000 km/h, the ISS figure).</p>`}});
G(2,"calc",()=>{const d=pick([384400,ri(55,400)*1e6]);const t=d*1e3/cL;return{q:`How long does a radio signal take to travel one way over <b>${d.toLocaleString()} km</b>${d===384400?" (Earth–Moon)":" (Earth–Mars at some point in its orbit)"}?`,a:t,unit:"s",e:`<p>t = d/c = ${sig(d*1e3,4)} / 3×10⁸ = <b>${sig(t,3)} s</b>${t>60?` (${sig(t/60,3)} min)`:""}. Long delays are why deep-space missions rely on onboard autonomy and IMUs.</p>`}});

// open
O(2,"open","(Tutorial 2 Q16) Explain the role of Payload Systems in mission success, considering both onboard instruments and ground equipment.","<ul><li>Onboard: scientific/remote-sensing instruments (SAR, radar altimeters, radiometers), communication payloads, navigation receivers (GPS/GLONASS/EGNOS/Galileo).</li><li>Ground segment: TT&C – telemetry, tracking, telecommand – plus signal coding, modulation and RF equipment.</li><li>Rising data rates need efficient coding/modulation, transponders and amplifiers.</li><li>Both together collect mission data and deliver it to mission control (science, EO, telecom).</li></ul>");ref("Tut 2 · Q16");
O(2,"open","(Tutorial 2 Q17) Describe the main satellite types and their functions, with an example each.","<ul><li>Communication – transmit/receive signals – Intelsat.</li><li>Weather – meteorological data – NOAA.</li><li>Navigation – positioning, navigation, timing – GPS.</li><li>Earth observation – images/data of surface, atmosphere, oceans – Landsat.</li><li>Scientific – research/experiments – Hubble.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 2 · Q17");
O(2,"open","(Tutorial 2 Q18) Discuss key features and applications of military satellites.","<ul><li>Surveillance & reconnaissance; secure (encrypted) communications; navigation & positioning; early warning (missile launches, nuclear tests); weather & environmental monitoring; navigation & targeting for precision strikes; cybersecurity & space security; multi-mission platforms; rapid deployment; constellations for resilience.</li><li>Details often classified.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 2 · Q18");
O(2,"open","(Tutorial 2 Q19) Elaborate on the ISS's collaborative nature and significance.","<ul><li>NASA, Roscosmos, JAXA, ESA, CSA – one of the largest international partnerships, crossing political/cultural/economic differences.</li><li>Habitable modular lab in microgravity: astrobiology, astronomy, meteorology, physics, human biology, materials.</li><li>~90 min orbit at ~28,000 km/h; occupied continuously since 2000; 6-month crews; STEM outreach.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 2 · Q19");
O(2,"open","(Tutorial 2 Q20) Examine advantages and challenges of NTP, CubeSats, solar sails and ion engines.","<ul><li>NTP: high thrust & efficiency for crewed Mars – safety, complexity, nuclear handling.</li><li>CubeSats: low cost, rapid development, constellations – limited payload, power, comms.</li><li>Solar sails: propellant-free long-duration propulsion – deployment, orientation.</li><li>Ion: very high efficiency (xenon), e.g. Dawn – low thrust, high power, complexity.</li></ul>");ref("Tut 2 · Q20");
O(2,"open","List the solar-radiation design considerations from the notes and give one design response for each.","<ul><li>Power generation → size and point solar arrays.</li><li>Thermal control → reflective surfaces, insulation, radiators.</li><li>Orbit position (LEO day/night cycling) → batteries.</li><li>Radiation pressure → include in trajectory planning.</li><li>Material degradation (UV) → choose resistant materials.</li><li>Solar storms/flares → protect electronics, safe modes.</li><li>Sun-angle constraints → array pointing.</li><li>Radiation hardening → rad-hard parts, shielding.</li><li>Thermal radiators → balance absorbed vs radiated.</li><li>Mission duration in darkness → energy storage.</li></ul>");
